hello --

i've put a new copy of vile up for ftp.  the latest version is 5.6.
it's in:
	ftp://id.wing.net/pub/pgf/vile/vile-5.6.tar.gz
it is also available from any mirror of the sunsite Linux archive,
down under apps/editors/vi.

it has been in use by quite a number of folks for at least a month, so
i think it's safe.  ;-)

i'm distributing this announcement pretty widely because vile builds
on a lot of platforms:
	- pretty much all UNIX systems, as either a "standard" or an X11 app.
	- VMS, on VAX or Alpha, again as "standard" or X11.
	- DOS, with memory extenders (e.g. DJGPP, or Watcom's dos4gw)
	- Win32 (i.e. Win95 or WinNT as a console app -- no menus, etc.)
	- OS/2 (as a console app.)
the other reason i'm announcing widely is that i've been told that
i've under-announced in the past.

for those that haven't heard of vile, here's a synopsis:

vile is a text editor.  it is extremely compatible with vi in terms of
"finger feel".  in addition, it has extended capabilities in many areas,
notably:
	multi-file editing and viewing
	key rebinding (in addition to :map, :map!, and :abbr)
	mouse support (in an xterm, or when built as xvile)
	infinite undo
	many additional operator commands
	selection highlighting
	rectangular operations
	"next error" cursor positioning after compilation
	full function- and arrow-key support
	filename, command, internal mode and variable completion
	command, search string, filename history
	auxiliary utilities for man page and C program syntax highlighting
	built-in macro language -- it's not beautiful, but it _is_ useful.
	portability to all UNIX platforms, VMS, DOS, NT, Win95, OS/2.

xvile is the X11 version of vile.  in addition to the capabilities
listed above for the character-oriented version of vile, xvile offers
scrollbars, color support, and more complete mouse integration.

i make pre-built binaries available for the PC operating systems on an
"as available" basis.

be warned that i _don't_ claim that vile is a _clone_.  it is _not_,
though it _is_ "extremely compatible".  if you want a more exact clone,
there are others available (like vim, and nvi).  but a lot of people
prefer vile.  you be the judge.

the name is a bad joke of an acronym: "VI Like Emacs", which
most people on both sides of the fence agree is a pretty vile concept.

