In the June issue of Linux Journal, we will be focusing on
"Linux in the Office", and we are looking for two kinds of
stories:

 o The Linux version of a tool being used to fill a vertical
   market niche (dentist's office, point of sale, etc.).
   It should be written by the vendor/person filling the niche.
   It should be reasonably specific to the niche in question.

 o How to use a product effectively in an office situation.
   This should focus on a relatively common, specific office
   task.  In other words, it shouldn't just be a friendly
   review that says "You can use this database/spreadsheet/
   whatever as a database/spreadsheet/whatever and it works."
   Something more like "Fast and easy mailing list administration
   with Foo for Linux" is more appropriate.

Please reply to this message or contact me, Michael K. Johnson,
at johnsonm@ssc.com

Because of our production schedule, and because we do not
enforce exact length restrictions, we cannot guarantee that an
article submission accepted for the June focus will absolutely
be in the June issue.  If it is not included in the June issue,
it will be kept for later publication.

michaelkjohnson



