-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Linuxconf 1.1 has been upload to sunsite.unc.edu. It is still sitting in
/pub/Linux/Incoming. It should move to /pub/Linux/system/admin.

I have upload

	linuxconf-1.1-src.tar.gz (Source)
	linuxconf-1.1.bin-aout.tar.gz (A.out for Slackware 2.x)
	linuxconf-1.1.bin-elf.tar.gz (ELF release running on Slackware 3.0)

Here are a list of changes since 1.1. I am also including a longer 
announce since the 1.0 one was broken (my mistake) and somewhat difficult 
to read.

What's new
==========

	LILO support

	All configuration file path can be changes dynamicly using the
	<edit> button of the screen "List all configuration files". This
	is handy for /etc/lilo.conf which has to be moved sometime.

	Cleanup to compile with GCC 2.7.0 and run on Slackware 3.0

	Some more work to PPP support. Add PLIP support. connect and disconnect
	feature work. Does not use the command line anymore to pass information
	to pppd to avoid showing password (using ps -axl).

	default runlevel configuration were changed to accomodate the new
	Slackware inittab (xdm mode has moved from runlevel 6 to 4).

	No longer need a special /sbin/init. Just place the line
	/sbin/askrunlevel at the end of /etc/rc.d/rc.S.
	It still work for with the modified /sbin/init (which was calling
	/sbin/askrunlevel directly). Even with this modified /sbin/init, you
	can safely place /sbin/askrunlevel at the end of rc.S.

	Start kerneld automaticly (on demand module loading now available
	since kernel 1.3.57)

	Load ethx devices as needed using modprobe

	Configure RARP and load the module as needed.

	Some bug fix in DNS management.

		Support CNAME field.
		Propagate glue record for subdomain into domain definition.

	Creation of POP mail only user accounts.



What is it anyway ?
===================


  Linuxconf 1.1
  Introduction


  This is the second release of Linuxconf, a new admin tool for Linux.
  After a year of development at Solucorp, we are quite proud of it.  It
  is available from sunsite.unc.edu as
  /pub/Linux/system/Admin/linuxconf-1.1.src.tar.gz
  /pub/Linux/system/Admin/linuxconf-1.1.bin-aout.tar.gz
  /pub/Linux/system/Admin/linuxconf-1.1.bin-elf.tar.gz

  1.  What is Linuxconf



  Linuxconf is a new admin tool for Linux. It goes much further than the
  simple set of user interface dialogs and script based utilities. It is
  a fully integrated utility which help you maintain a working Linux
  system without difficulties.

  Linuxconf knows how to manage quite a few things in your system.


  2.  Why is it "hot"


  Linuxconf is both a configurator and an activator. It is the one which
  decide if ...


  o  A daemon must be started.

  o  A configuration command must be executed.

  o  A daemon must be killed/restarted.

  o  A daemon must be signaled (kill -HUP ...).

  o  A network interface must be (re)configured.

  o  A network route must be set/remove.

  Linuxconf act based on configuration files. It knows about most Linux
  configuration files and can compare their status with what is running
  currently. Based on this query, Linuxconf can bring your system
  "current".

  Whether you use Linuxconf's user interface or edit the configuration
  files by hand is unimportant. The following command


                       linuxconf --status




  will give you a report of what has to be done to bring the system
  "current".


                       linuxconf --update





  will do it for you!

  Rebooting is simply not an option anymore. This is really its main
  goal. Every time you quit Linuxconf, it will probe all configuration
  files and make sure your workstation is "current". If it is not, it
  will tell you what have to be done and do it (you are allowed not to
  do it).


  3.  Linuxconf takes control very early


  One component of linuxconf is called "askrunlevel".  It takes control
  very early. A call to /sbin/askrunlevel must be placed at the end of
  your /etc/rc.d/rc.S file.  This utility allows you to select a proper
  runlevel (logical operation mode) or to completly reconfigured your
  system even before the boot sequence is finished.

  From this utility, you are allowed to ...


  o  Start Linux in graphical mode (xdm) and fully activate the
     networking.

  o  Go graphic, without networking.

  o  Run as an X terminal.

  o  Start in text mode, full networking.

  o  Text mode, no network.

  o  Configure the workstation.

  Linuxconf allows you to configure the workstation even before it is
  fully booted. This is a winner as it saves you from false start boot
  (often with an invalid network configuration).


  4.  What Linuxconf knows so far


  Linuxconf knows how to configure and managed the following things.


  o  System time, time zone, CMOS clock.

  o  LILO management.

  o  Basic networking configuration (IP numbers and net mask) of the
     four Ethernets adaptors.

  o  Static routing. Default route.

  o  Filesystems (/etc/fstab).

  o  Routed daemon.

  o  NIS client (ypbind).

  o  NFS server.

  o  PPP client configuration and activation.

  o  Users and groups.

  o  The named daemon. Yes Linuxconf provides a friendly interface to
     configure and maintain a DNS. Check this out!

  o  Sendmail. Check this out!

  o  Fire-walling (Packet filtering).

  o  RARP setup.

  Further, Linuxconf provide a friendly interface to configure theses
  files.


  o  /etc/fstab

  o  /etc/host.conf

  o  /etc/hosts

  o  /etc/networks

  o  /etc/resolv.conf


  5.  Command line mode


  Linuxconf provide a command line mode allowing better integration with
  shell scripts. Here is a sample


  o  netconf --runlevel local | client | server

  o  netconf --status

  o  netconf --update

  o  netconf --connect pppaccount

  o  dnsconf --set host ip-number ...

  o  dnsconf --unset host

  The dnsconf utility should be easily integrated with the DHCP daemon,
  allowing full synchronization with the DNS.


  6.  Current state


  Linuxconf is now usable and already a big time saver.

  Linuxconf install over any standard Slackware system includind the
  latest 3.0.  Just UN-tar linuxconf-1.1.bin-aout.tar.gz (or bin-elf) in
  the root and execute /install/doinst.sh. Read carefully what is
  written.

  Linuxconf may work with other distribution (it should in fact).  I
  have no experience with other than Slackware (2.x and 3.0) at this
  point, so I can only recommend Linuxconf to really experienced users
  (Using something different than Slackware).

  This is the second public release. Many glitches has been cured.



  7.  Documentation

  Linuxconf has online help (context) for most screen.  (45 out of 52
  help screen are done). This represent a lot of work. Comments and some
  help are welcome.


  8.  Technology


  Linuxconf is written in C++. It currently has a ncurses interface. It
  scores at around 30,000 lines of code.

  It currently compiled with GCC 2.6.2 and GCC 2.7.0.  For GCC 2.6.2,
  define the environement variable OLDGCC


                       export OLDGCC=on





  9.  Future

  Many more things are expect to be included in Linuxconf.  Here are few
  of them.


  o  Graphical user interface..

  o  Inittab, and getty/mgetty management.

  o  inetd.conf

  o  Samba management.

  o  sudo management.

  o  bootp, DHCP, rarp.

  o  uucp configuration.

  o  httpd server configuration (Apache probably).


  10.  Licensing

  Linuxconf is distributed under the GNU GPL license.  See the file
  LICENSE included in the distribution.

  Like most other Linux components, there are very little restrictions
  about usage and redistribution. We would like to hear from
  distribution maintainers. Linuxconf is certainly a key candidate to
  ease Linux acceptance.  Comments are welcome!


  11.  Author


  Linuxconf was written at Solucorp by Jacques Gelinas
  (jacques@solucorp.qc.ca). Solucorp is installing Linuxconf to customer
  site for some time now.  Solucorp is a consulting company doing work
  in the following fields.


  o  Linux!

  o  Networking (Internet services, fire-walling).

  o  Multi-platform software development.

- --------------------------------------------------------
Jacques Gelinas (jacques@solucorp.qc.ca)
