-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----



There is a commercial network client/server based product called
Networker (from Legato) systems.

The server portion exists on several different server platforms
(managing indexes and backup media and jukeboxes):  SunOS, Solaris,
Solaris/86, Solaris/PPC, AIX, SCO, HPUX, Netware, plus several others.

The client portion exists on many more platforms: Dos, Windows,
Windows NT, OS/2, about 15-20 different Unix flavors.

I have produced a linux client version, which I can make the binaries
only (sorry) available for free.

Here's the scoop/limitations:

	+ You'll have to have a Networker server somewhere in your
	  net that you can add more clients to.

	+ This Linux version does *not* have the usual graphical
	  interfaces (the Unix Networker client s/w is Motif based,
	  and I haven't purchased Mooteef or re-ported to OW or
	  Athena...). It *does* have all the usual client command
	  line interfaces, including the ability to have automatic
	  save groups invoked by the remote server.

	+ I and a few other people have been using this product lightly
	  for some months, but no real formal testing has been done.
	  Use at your own risk!!!!!!!!!!!!!!

	+ Support: I am an employee of Legato Systems, but this particular
	  release of software is *not* directly approved by Legato Systems.
	  Do *not* call them up with questions about this client support-
	  Tech Support swore they would <fill in your favporite dire threat
	  here> if I persisted in sharing this outside the company. I have
	  the tacit approval of some individuals in the company that this
	  work can be a shared, unsupported, binary only release.

	  Please send any mail questions, flames, bug reports, to either
	  me (mjacob@feral.com, or mjacob@legato.com), or to
	  linux@legato.com

	+ Please purchase Legato server licences and say it's to support
	  the linux and/or a freebsd client port- that will legitimize
	  any time I spend on this.

Where to get the binaries:

	feral.com anonymous ftp:pub/linux/networker.tar.gz

	legato.com anonymous ftp:pub/Unsupported/Linux_Client
	(this seems to come and go at times- I don't control that machine)



I thought long and hard about whether to release a binary only
product, when I would much prefer to release a very reduced feature
source product. However, this was the best compromise I could reach
with a number of folks about how to get more client support out there.

I essentially did this port so I could back up *my* linux machines
to an external server, and am glad to share this feature with folks
who can use it as well.




