SOFTWARE RELEASE ANNOUNCEMENT : January 8. 1996
 Title:  shak
 Version: 1.00

Shak is an entry in the distributed, network transparent 
Backup category.  

Shak is a program, for creating tape backups, that 
encapsulates an arbitrary user backup program
with the restriction that the user backup produces 
and one (1) tape file.

Shak alternates the user backup files with shak header
tape files and maintains the headers in a disk file for
quick access.  The header information identifies the 
backup and supports implementation of full, differential, 
and incremental backup types by entering information in 
the user backup programs environment as defined variables.

Shak is a command line (interface) shell (sh) program.  

Title: shak
Version: 1.00 
Origin: original work by the author of this announcement.
Release state: first non-beta release
Author: James Lowe, Jr. <jhl@richmond.infi.net>
Copying Policy: GNU GPL.

Distribution file: shak-1.00.tar.gz  (74 Kbytes)

Where to get shak: ftp at sunsite.unc.edu and mirror sites.
	           /pub/Linux/Incoming   or
		   /pub/Linux/system/Backup

Features of interest: 
    * Shak manages tape cartridges (by tape ID number) and 
      manages tape files on a cartridge by maintaining a tape 
      index file, for each tape, in the file system.
    * Shak is network transparent in all of its operations.
    * Supports "shakless" clients. i.e will execute a program
      in a defined environment on a remote host with
      environment variables exported by shak.  
    * All tape headers are plain ascii text.
    * Shak is programmed in shell (sh) and standard 
      Unix programs; and employs  standard tape files 
      that mt(1) knows how to read.
    * No special kernel or networking features or 
      drivers are required.
    * Includes a file list filtering program that parses
      a date string. ( based on GNU RCS partime.c function)
    * Does NOT impose the form of the backup tape file or the
      backup program, although it does presume the existence of
      a RCS Id string in the backup program.

Shak comes complete with some example backup scripts 
that provide some decent features.

Here is the first and last pages of the section 8 man page.


SHAK(8)							  SHAK(8)


NAME
       shak  -	Manage	tape  files  and make network transparent
       backups.

SYNOPSIS
       shak [ -adgiklmqtuvxy ] subcommand [args ...]

DESCRIPTION
       Shak is a Bourne shell program  system  that  positions	a
       tape  at	 "end-of-recorded-media" and runs a user-supplied
       backup program, among  other  functions	described  below.
       Shak defines and manages a tape file metaformat specifica-
       tion (One or more tape files of prescribed  content  on	a
       single  tape).	A tape file, according to shak may either
       be an ascii text header or the output of the user-supplied
       backup  program.	  Shak	creates, tests, and enforces this
       tape file metaformat.

       The tape format is as follows.  A header begins every tape
       and  precedes  every  user-backup  tape	file on the tape,
       hence headers are file numbers zero (first)  and	 all  odd
       numbered	 tape files.  User backup files are all even num-
       bered tape files.  Shak does not	 impose	 a  form  on  nor
       alter the contents of even numbered tape files.	Shak does
       impose the constraint that the user backup program  writes
       exactly	one  (1)  tape	file.	The  tape headers contain
       information to identify the origin and date  of	the  tape
       (tape  file  zero)  and	each  succeeding user-backup tape
       file.  (tape files 1,3,5,...)  This  information	 supports
       the  implementation of full, differential, and incremental
       backup types.

       The scope of shak is only that of  a  tape  file	 manager,
       many  features  must  be	 implemented by the user-supplied
       backup program.	 The  shak  distribution  provides  shell
       scripts	using shell variables exported by shak to achieve
       a full-featured, structured backup  system.   Shak  stores
       the  tape headers (zeroth and subsequent odd numbered tape
       files) in a tape index file thus reducing tape read opera-
       tions  normally required to retrieve information stored in
       the odd numbered tape files.  A date-string  parsing  file
       list  filter  program  is provided in the source distribu-
       tion.  A means for logging backed up files to  a	 file  on
       any   host   is	supported  through  the	 methods  of  the
       user-supplied backup program.  Shak uses the 'fsf', 'eom',
       'rewind'	 and  'bsf' options of the mt(1) command and uses
       the remote  shell  program,  rsh(1),  to	 achieve  network
       transparency.


SHAK(8)							  SHAK(8)


FILES
       /usr/etc/shak/shak.conf
	      The site-wide shak configuration file.
       /etc/shak/shak.conf
	      The machine local shak configuration file.
       ${SHAK_INDEXPATH}/INDEX_${SHAK_DOMAIN}_${SHAK_TAPENUMBER}
	      The tape index file.
       ${SHAK_INDEXPATH}/INDEX_${SHAK_DOMAIN}_${SHAK_TAPENUMBER}.bak
	      The old previous tape index file.

SEE ALSO
       shak(5),	 mtimefilter(1),  mt(1), sh(1), ksh(1), ident(1),
       shak/script/backupshak.sh

BUGS
       Shak does not respond  to  signals  elegantly;  and,  only
       knows  of  tape	errors	through	 mt(1) or other program's
       stderr file.  Shak is unaware of some   error  conditions.
       Tape  headers  are  not ANSI standard compliant, if one is
       applicable.  Shak is a shell  program,  and  it	is  slow.
       Shak offers no help toward restoration of a shak backup.

- -----------end of announcement---------------------------------------------

