    meeting attendees are free to take any distributions they would
    like to try from the library, and are encouraged to donate
    unneeded distributions.  The library's goal is to encourage
    experimentation with Linux for those who have not yet tried it,
    and to enable easy trial of unfamiliar distributions.
    
    Don Lewis is currently the CLUE Librarian.


Presenters Needed
- -----------------

    We would like your ideas for presenters/speakers, and topics for
    both Meeting presentations, and also Newsletter articles.  Please
    help; supply either or both for us.  Volunteers are especially
    appreciated.


Notes From Last Meeting (November 9, 1995)
- ------------------------------------------
    by Mark Bolzern
    
    Presented at the November 9, 1995 meeting, was the topic Linux at
    Work, presented by Mark Bolzern.
    
    Using evidence from WorkGroup Solutions customer files, Mark
    stated that Linux is now in use virtually all Fortune 2000
    companies, and yet management for the most part still asks
    "What's Linux?"
    
    Just like the original IBM PC and it's clones, which set the
    standard for computer hardware, Linux is so inexpensive, and so
    useful that people bring it in to get the job done, whatever that
    is.  In corporate culture it is easier to just do something, and
    apologize later than it is to get permission.
    
    Linux is following exactly the same acceptance model that the IBM
    PC did in the early 80s.  People bring a product into a company
    because it helps them accomplish what they need to do.  No one
    but immediate co-workers know about it.  Management generally is
    not told at all. Then co-workers start seeing how useful it is,
    and use it too.  Then because of pressure from workers, immediate
    supervisors get involved and departmental funds are used. 
    Finally MIS and upper management figure it out, and then are at a
    loss as to how to deal with it.  This is how corporate America is
    being overrun by Linux, because it is less expensive, and more
    useful than what Microsoft has to offer.  Like all true
    revolutions, it is starting underground, among students, with the
    rank and file people, and has only a few significantly visible
    leaders.
    
    Just like the IBM PC set the open standard for hardware, Linux is
    in the process of setting the open standard for the Operating
    Systems Level.
    
    Mark reiterated a fair amount of history, and drew parallels to
    the events in the history of Linux to prove his points.  Mark
    participated materially in the release and acceptance of the IBM
    PC, as well as being one of the founders of the Internet.  Mark
    was involved in writing the original ethernet packet protocols,
    and making some of the original connections in the early 70s, and
    has continued to participate ever since.  A more complete
    biography, along with a writeup on the significance of Linux is
    available in the file 
    ftp://ftp.wgs.com/pub2/wgs/lxsig.txt
    
    After this introduction, Mark opened the interactive part of the
    meeting by asking people in the audience to tell their stories
    about how they use Linux at work.  Quite number of people spoke
    up about how they were using Linux systems as X-Terminals at
    work, or to access their work systems from home, for E-Mail, for
    Internet news, Graphics, PC-Lan server, for learning Unix, and
    for fun.  Among other uses, the use of Linux as a general
    workstation or as a general server instead of much higher priced
    Unix boxes, or crash prone PCs seemed common.
    
    These Linux systems generally sat on someone's desk serving as a
    workstation, but often did server tasks at the same time.
    
    In most cases management had not known of the use of Linux till
    well after the systems were installed.  In some cases
    justification had to be made to management first... and sometimes
    Management did not believe that copying one CD onto many machines
    was legitimate.  In almost all cases the user of Linux said that
    the Linux system was less expensive than the alternatives, and
    generally did a better job.  Mostly people learned of Linux from
    a friend, who in turn had done the same.
    
    A number of people said that having the source code to the OS
    available, and access to the developers of the product on the
    Internet was far better tech support than they obtained from
    manufacturers that they had dealt with.
    
    All in all it was an excellent session, and Mark wants to thank
    everyone that came, and especially those that spoke up and
    participated.
    

